# How we write code

- Production-quality, maintainable code.
- Get things done with a Refactor To Pattern mindset (Martin Fowler).
- Clarity over cleverness — the simple, obvious solution is usually better.
- Feature branch mentality — no backwards compatibility needed.

When stuck: **stop**, re-read requirements, ask: "I see approaches [A] vs [B] — which do you prefer?"

## Checkpoints

Stop and validate: after completing a feature, before starting a new component, when something feels wrong, before declaring "done".

## Done Means

- All linters pass (zero issues)
- All tests pass
- Feature works end-to-end
- Old code is deleted (always delete replaced code)

## Testing

- Complex business logic → tests first
- Hot paths → benchmarks
- No premature optimization — measure before claiming faster
- Load `nvm` before running TypeScript tests/code

## Code Style

- Function names should be self-documenting
- Keep comments minimal — don't repeat what the code says
- Only add comments for non-obvious logic or important context
- Comments explain **why**, not **what**
- No barrel files (index.ts) — import directly from source files

## Git

- Never add Co-Authored-By lines to commit messages

## Working Memory

When context gets long: re-read this file, summarize progress in PROGRESS.md, document state before major changes.

## Project Setup

- If the project contains AGENT.md files, read them
- @~/.claude/local.md for local overrides
